World jogs of X, Y and Z along the tilted work plane and along the tool.

5axiskins with a 100 mm pivot, the tool centre point type, Y slow and
short against X and Z.  The plane frame: a plane defined under an XY
rotation, incremental jogs of each letter, each world move checked
against the plane's axes as status reports them turned by the XY
rotation, rotaries untouched; a machine jog afterwards starts where the
frame jogs left the machine.  A plane whose X runs mostly along Y: the
jog along it runs at the speed Y allows.  One frame axis moves at a
time: a jog of plane Y asked for while plane X runs waits until X has
stopped.  A continuous jog stops where the turned axis meets the
[AXIS_Y] travel without a soft limit fault, plane Y asked for at once
too, and no further jog leaves it.  The frame cannot change while a jog
is under way.  The tool frame with the head laid over and turned: the
jogs follow the tool frame status reports, and with a plane in effect
the tool's X is turned about the tool axis as near the plane's X as it
goes.  Under the identity type the tool frame is the machine's.  Every servo
cycle of every jog is sampled: no world axis goes over its own speed or
acceleration.  The whole test runs on the trapezoid and on the S-curve
planner.
